WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.638
Microsoft Learn's Intune migration guide
points to Microsoft's Intune Graph samples,

2
00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:08.787
on GitHub, for exporting
and importing policies,

3
00:00:08.787 --> 00:00:12.250
and says the scripts change
your tenant and come as-is.

4
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.275
Their settings catalog
folder has an import script.

5
00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:17.864
The repository keeps a pinned copy,

6
00:00:17.864 --> 00:00:22.150
and a test harness runs it,
unmodified, against a fake Graph.

7
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.537
Here's what that shows.

8
00:00:23.537 --> 00:00:27.288
The import creates the policy
with one POST, unassigned.

9
00:00:27.288 --> 00:00:28.663
That's kept as the model.

10
00:00:28.663 --> 00:00:32.573
But run it twice and it creates a
second policy with the same name,

11
00:00:32.573 --> 00:00:34.638
because it never reads the tenant.

12
00:00:34.638 --> 00:00:39.342
It sends to the worldwide Graph host
whatever cloud you signed in to, and Learn's

13
00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:44.263
page on Microsoft Graph national cloud
deployments gives each cloud its own host.

14
00:00:44.263 --> 00:00:47.788
It uses whatever session you
opened, whatever its scopes.

15
00:00:47.788 --> 00:00:53.263
It silently strips service-set properties,
so the body sent isn't the file reviewed.

16
00:00:53.263 --> 00:00:55.688
And it records nothing beyond the screen.

17
00:00:55.688 --> 00:01:01.002
The example adds a sync that reads the
tenant first, a host per cloud, a read-only

18
00:01:01.002 --> 00:01:05.950
scope for the dry run, refusals instead
of silent edits, and a sync record.
